@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1194 = private unnamed_addr constant [1410 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `except` clauses that catch all exceptions.  This includes\0Abare `except`, `except BaseException` and `except Exception`.\0A\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AOverly broad `except` clauses can lead to unexpected behavior, such as\0Acatching `KeyboardInterrupt` or `SystemExit` exceptions that prevent the\0Auser from exiting the program.\0A\0AInstead of catching all exceptions, catch only those that are expected to\0Abe raised in the `try` block.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    foo()\0Aexcept BaseException:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    foo()\0Aexcept FileNotFoundError:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0AExceptions that are re-raised will _not_ be flagged, as they're expected to\0Abe caught elsewhere:\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    foo()\0Aexcept BaseException:\0A    raise\0A```\0A\0AExceptions that are logged via `logging.exception()` or `logging.error()`\0Awith `exc_info` enabled will _not_ be flagged, as this is a common pattern\0Afor propagating exception traces:\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    foo()\0Aexcept BaseException:\0A    logging.exception(\22Something went wrong\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: The `try` statement](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-try-statement)\0A- [Python documentation: Exception hierarchy](https://docs.python.org/3/library/exceptions.html#exception-hierarchy)\0A- [PEP 8: Programming Recommendations on bare `except`](https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/#programming-recommendations)\0A", align 1
