@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1396 = private unnamed_addr constant [852 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `print` statements that use the `>>` syntax.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python 2, the `print` statement can be used with the `>>` syntax to\0Aprint to a file-like object. This `print >> sys.stderr` syntax no\0Alonger exists in Python 3, where `print` is only a function, not a\0Astatement.\0A\0AInstead, use the `file` keyword argument to the `print` function, the\0A`sys.stderr.write` function, or the `logging` module.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom __future__ import print_function\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aprint >> sys.stderr, \22Hello, world!\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aprint(\22Hello, world!\22, file=sys.stderr)\0A```\0A\0AOr:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.stderr.write(\22Hello, world!\\n\22)\0A```\0A\0AOr:\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Alogging.error(\22Hello, world!\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `print`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#print)\0A", align 1
