@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1489 = private unnamed_addr constant [1388 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for incorrect usages of `default_factory` as a keyword argument when\0Ainitializing a `defaultdict`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `defaultdict` constructor accepts a callable as its first argument.\0AFor example, it's common to initialize a `defaultdict` with `int` or `list`\0Avia `defaultdict(int)` or `defaultdict(list)`, to create a dictionary that\0Areturns `0` or `[]` respectively when a key is missing.\0A\0AThe default factory _must_ be provided as a positional argument, as all\0Akeyword arguments to `defaultdict` are interpreted as initial entries in\0Athe dictionary. For example, `defaultdict(foo=1, bar=2)` will create a\0Adictionary with `{\22foo\22: 1, \22bar\22: 2}` as its initial entries.\0A\0AAs such, `defaultdict(default_factory=list)` will create a dictionary with\0A`{\22default_factory\22: list}` as its initial entry, instead of a dictionary\0Athat returns `[]` when a key is missing. Specifying a `default_factory`\0Akeyword argument is almost always a mistake, and one that type checkers\0Acan't reliably detect.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as converting `default_factory` from a\0Akeyword to a positional argument will change the behavior of the code, even\0Aif the keyword argument was used erroneously.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adefaultdict(default_factory=int)\0Adefaultdict(default_factory=list)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adefaultdict(int)\0Adefaultdict(list)\0A```\0A", align 1
